# evidence-for: US-006, US-007, FR-019, FR-023, FR-039, AC-039
#
# The sweep D-149 asks for: every positional or relative-location clause in
# tests/test_survey.py and tests/test_llmstxt.py re-read against the files as
# they stand, and measured rather than reasoned about.
#
# Section 1 is the population -- every line in either module carrying one of
# below, above, beside, next to, beneath, underneath, immediately, adjacent,
# preceding, following, or a line count. It is printed whole so a reader can
# see what the sweep had in front of it rather than only what it concluded.
#
# Section 2 is the part of that population that names another passage of the
# same module. Each row states which way the clause says the named passage
# lies, finds the nearest line actually holding it, and prints "holds" or
# "STALE". All 19 hold at this commit. The one that did not is D-149's, which
# names a passage of scripts/survey.py rather than of this module and is
# measured in evidence/casting-4-decorator-guard-position.log.
#
# Section 3 is the rest: clauses that name a position in the scripts, where a
# grep of this module can never catch a drift because the thing that moves is
# in the other file.
#   a. "the `@` line and six lines after it" (test_survey.py) and "under the
#      same six-line cap" (the flag scan): both joins are `lines[i:i + 6]`,
#      at survey.py 284 and 613.
#   b. "this pass counts parens in the raw text where the decorator pass counts
#      through `call_depth_delta`": the decorator join calls call_depth_delta
#      at 277 and 286; the flag join counts args.count("(") at 612 and 615.
#   c. "the run of `#` lines directly above `import tomllib`": the comment run
#      ends on the line before the import in both scripts, with no blank
#      between. This one is also asserted at runtime by
#      assert_floor_comment_sits_on_the_import, so it cannot go stale silently.
#   d. "the first read of it below is `pkg.get`": survey.py loads pkg at 120
#      and first reads it at 128, which is a `.get`.
#   e. "the comment above the header chain" / "the comment above the chain":
#      the comment runs 258-270 and the chain it describes is 271-274, so the
#      comment is above it. The same clause's count -- five authored sources
#      cleaned through one_line, the checkout's directory name the sixth and
#      not cleaned -- is measured as five one_line() calls in those three
#      lines, with os.path.basename(ROOT) standing outside them.
#   f. "its `methods=["POST"]` sits one line past where the read stops": the
#      flaskcap fixture opens its decorator on its line 6, the join reads six
#      lines after that (7 through 12), and methods= is on 13.
#
# Two clauses were read and deliberately left alone. tests/test_survey.py:76
# and :1486 both say "the count above it was not touched"; cycle 14 measured
# that clause under the reading "the count stated above in this note", which is
# where the count sits in both places, and bound it in
# evidence/casting-4-attribution-measured.log. Rewriting prose that has already
# been measured is churn, so it stands.
#
# No script is touched by this commit; the diff is one docstring in
# tests/test_survey.py. See evidence/casting-4-tests-green.log.
#
# Every grep in this log takes one file, with the name prefixed by sed rather
# than by grep. `grep` on the machine this was written on is a shell function
# wrapping ugrep, which searches multiple file arguments in parallel and emits
# them in whichever order they finish, so a two-file grep made the log stop
# reproducing itself between runs.
#
